From 312bea9ec0ac099375a24c450d3c584c8c5a45fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 20:35:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Price item 4-2's arithmetic half, and refute it along with
 the lead it points at

The item says "arithmetic -> raw double/int where feedback says so" and has been
open on one stated blocker: the numeric-vs-generic signal was left uncollected
because the compiler already proves numeric-ness statically, so a runtime counter
has to be defined against that to say anything new. Defined that way, the
population is exactly item 3-1's NoSavingToMake refusal -- a single-node
arithmetic tree over two unprovable leaves, 26.22% of the corpus's candidate
nodes and the whole of 3-1's compile-time residue -- which 3-1 refuses because
the guarded form removes no box. That argument is about allocation and says
nothing about time. This asks what it removes instead.

The census the item would rest on was a seven-suite figure and it does not
survive widening. arithmeticBothNumbers read 100.00%, quoted as "the guard
predicts"; over the twelve suites that run it is 92.10% of 26.2 M, and the
per-suite spread is 0.46% on Splay against 100.00% on four others. Third
instrument to fall to the same thing section 4.2a found, and the first where the
spread rather than the total is the finding: a static widening would be paying
the miss on millions of operations.

Priced with a three-arm probe -- generic, guarded-hit, guarded-miss -- run
round-robin with the arms reversed on alternate rounds and ratioed within each
round. Multiply is 0.704x, 11 of 12 rounds, 6.39 ns saved. Add is 0.906x, and
its miss is 18.567x because the failure is a string concatenation rather than a
coercion. Relational is 0.753x, 12 of 12, and its miss is still 0.917x -- the
one guard here with no losing side.

Multiplied out, the item is refused by its own arithmetic. Best case over the
corpus is 147 ms of a 391 572 ms run, 0.038%; at the add rate it is net
negative. The landed 4-2b was 0.83% and the roadmap already called that below
the noise floor.

The relational lead it points at is closed the same way rather than left as a
guess, which needed the counter nobody had built: no fast path in this engine
reaches a comparison over two boxed operands, since 3-1's speculation excludes
relational operators and 3-5 needs one side already unboxed. Counted, 23 986 595
comparisons at 99.85% both-Numbers -- far more uniform than arithmetic -- and
worth 23 ms, 0.006%.

So the whole generic binary-operator surface, 50.2 M invocations, is 498 ms of
391 572 ms: 0.127% of the corpus's execution time if it were removed entirely.

The relational counter is counted once per source-level comparison by a
re-entrancy guard rather than by a case analysis over which paths delegate --
that analysis is what left BitwiseXor unhooked and silent about it -- and four
fixtures assert exact counts, including through the re-dispatching object path
where a naive hook double counts, and across all four operators so a missing
hook shows as three. Hooks sit behind the existing off-by-default flag on the
same methods whose arithmetic siblings already carry one.

Compiler suite green: 1 267 tests.
